{
  "requirement": "Habeas Codicem supports in-place conversion of legacy evidence to the versioned\nrepresentation under {cite}`requirements.sdk.habeas.evidence_format`, preserving\nthe historical values of its judgments, rationales, recorded inputs, and all\nreview-specific fields.",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "**Obligations from the subject text.** Legacy evidence converts in place to the versioned representation. The conversion preserves historical judgments (`determination`), rationales, recorded inputs, and all review-specific fields. The in-place, atomic, idempotent, writes, provenance and legacy_freshness sub-obligations belong to refining requirements. Those have their own tests, so I did not reassess them here.\n\n**Coverage.**\n- `src/evidence_format.rs#mod[tests]/fn[legacy_conversion_preserves_every_historical_field_without_resolving_sources]` covers all four review kinds.\n  - The kinds are requirement, example (with its `example` key), citation (roles and commentary), and site (queries, applies, fixmes). The `legacy` helper builds each fixture.\n  - The test asserts `record.version == None` and `record.output == old`, so every field is preserved verbatim. It also checks the rationale explicitly.\n  - It asserts the input dependencies, and it asserts the input is not resolved, since the file `deleted.rs` no longer exists.\n  - It encodes to the native form and checks `format_version`, `review_version`, and `view(&native) == old`.\n  - It checks that decoding the native form gives the same dependencies, and that the native form holds no `source text`.\n- `src/evidence_format.rs#mod[tests]/fn[legacy_true_false_and_fixme_determinations_preserve_the_application_answer]` covers the three historical determination forms: true, false, and FIXME.\n  - For each, it asserts output equality, no injected `result` or `metadata` keys, the round trip through `view`, and re-decoding of the native form.\n  - The false case also shows that older negative records without a correction list stay readable.\n- `src/evidence_format.rs#mod[tests]/fn[atomic_conversion_replaces_the_file_and_never_exposes_partial_bytes]` is marked only for the atomic sub-requirement. It shows that `convert_file` writes exactly `encode(decode(old))` to the file. Combined with the `view` round-trip assertions above, this ties the on-disk in-place conversion to the preserved values.\n\n**Residual considerations.**\n- The legacy fixture uses the outline and requirements input kinds, not the `definition` input kind. The conversion carries the whole recorded input through unchanged in the output, and the output-equality assertions cover that. Dependency freshness is the job of the `legacy_freshness` sub-requirement, so this is not a gap here.\n- I found no gap and no marking gap.",
  "references": "4105227098deece910b14872cc5915eaaa745538be30892f53f87fcae3b28cf6",
